  "text": "Let's denote the first term of the arithmetic sequence as \\( a_1 \\) and the common difference as \\( d \\). From the problem, we know:\n\n1. The first term \\( a_1 = 5 \\).\n2. The fourth term \\( a_4 = 20 \\).\n\nThe formula for the \\( n \\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n\\[ a_n = a_1 + (n-1)d \\]\n\nFor the fourth term:\n\\[ a_4 = 5 + 3d = 20 \\]\n\nFrom this equation, we can solve for \\( d \\):\n\\[ 5 + 3d = 20 \\]\n\\[ 3d = 15 \\]\n\\[ d = 5 \\]\n\nThe common difference \\( d \\) is 5. So the arithmetic sequence is:\n\\[ 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, \\ldots \\]\n\nNow we need to form a new geometric sequence by removing the seventh term (which is 25). The new sequence is:\n\\[ 5, 10, 15, 20, 25 \\]\n\nThe first term of the new geometric sequence is \\( a_1' = 5 \\). The common ratio \\( r \\) of the geometric sequence is:\n\\[ r = \\frac{a_2'}{a_1'} = \\frac{10}{5} = 2 \\]\n\\[ r = \\frac{a_3'}{a_2'} = \\frac{15}{10} = \\frac{3}{2} \\]\n\\[ r = \\frac{a_4'}{a_3'} = \\frac{20}{15} = \\frac{4}{3} \\]\n\\[ r = \\frac{a_5'}{a_4'} = \\frac{25}{20} = \\frac{5}{4} \\]\n\nThe common ratio \\( r \\) of the new geometric sequence should be consistent. Therefore, we check if the ratio between consecutive terms is the same:\n\n\\[ \\frac{10}{5} = 2 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{15}{10} = \\frac{3}{2} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{20}{15} = \\frac{4}{3} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{25}{20} = \\frac{5}{4} \\]\n\nSince the ratios are not the same, we need to reconsider the problem. The correct interpretation is that the common ratio of the new geometric sequence is the reciprocal of the common ratio of the arithmetic sequence, which is the ratio of the seventh term to the first term of the arithmetic sequence.\n\nThe seventh term of the arithmetic sequence is:\n\\[ a_7 = 5 + 6d = 5 + 6 \\cdot 5 = 5 + 30 = 35 \\]\n\nThe common ratio \\( r \\) of the new geometric sequence is:\n\\[ r = \\frac{35}{5} = 7 \\]\n\nThus, the common ratio of the new geometric sequence is:\n\\[ \\boxed{7} \\]",
